From: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: cix: acpi-resource-lookup: bound the readiness wait so consumers cannot defer forever

cix_acpi_resource_lookup_ready()/cix_acpi_resource_lookup_defer() only
ever publish readiness from inside acpi_resource_lookup_probe()
(CIXA1019's own .probe()). If that probe never runs at all - CIXA1019
is absent from a given platform's ACPI namespace, its own driver
registration failed, or (should CONFIG_CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP ever
become tristate, as the header's IS_REACHABLE() guard already
anticipates) its module is simply never loaded - readiness is never
published, and any consumer (CIX PHY/CDNSP drivers) relying on
cix_acpi_resource_lookup_defer() to turn a transient -ENOENT into
-EPROBE_DEFER during the injection window instead defers forever once
it hits a *permanent* -ENOENT that CIXA1019 was never going to satisfy.
The previous patch already correctly named this deferral "bounded" in
its description, but nothing in the code actually bounded it.

Add an explicit, bounded fallback:

 - A late_initcall() (acpi_resource_lookup_late_init()) schedules a
   delayed work item once boot has reached the point where every
   built-in driver has had its initial probe attempt, if readiness is
   not already published by then.
 - The work item (acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_work()) checks
   acpi_dev_present("CIXA1019", NULL, -1): if the device is not
   present at all, readiness is published immediately - nothing will
   ever inject properties on this platform, so there is nothing left
   to defer for.
 - If CIXA1019 *is* present but still has not published readiness, the
   work reschedules itself up to CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_MAX_WAIT_RETRIES
   (20) times at CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_WAIT_INTERVAL (250 ms)
   intervals (5 seconds total), then forces readiness anyway with a
   loud dev_warn()-equivalent pr_warn(), on the reasoning that an
   unbounded consumer defer is strictly worse than surfacing whatever
   real error consumers hit at that point - a CIXA1019 that is present
   but has not completed its (unconditionally-successful,
   never-deferring) probe after 5 seconds indicates a genuine problem
   elsewhere that this mechanism cannot fix, and should not be allowed
   to masquerade as "everything is fine, keep waiting".
 - These two constants are a software boot-policy choice (how long to
   wait during boot before giving up on a stuck/absent provider), not
   a hardware timing requirement, and are documented as such - unlike
   PHY/reset sequencing delays, they do not model any register or
   electrical characteristic and were not reverse-engineered from any
   vendor source; they were chosen to be generously long for a one-time
   boot-time wait while still bounding it to a human-noticeable-but-not
   -indefinite window.

This does not change behavior for the common, working case (CIXA1019
present and probing normally): its probe still runs from the existing
subsys_initcall-registered platform_driver exactly as before, still
publishes readiness via the existing smp_store_release() as soon as
injection completes, and the late_initcall's own delayed work exits
immediately (no-op) once it observes readiness already true.

Signed-off-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Change-Id: I0085000000000000000000000000000000000085
---
 drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c | 73 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 73 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c b/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c
@@ -21,12 +21,14 @@
 
 #include <linux/acpi.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
+#include <linux/jiffies.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.h>
 #include <linux/uuid.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 static struct acpi_device *acpi_obj_to_adev(const union acpi_object *obj)
 {
@@ -638,6 +640,77 @@ bool cix_acpi_resource_lookup_ready(void)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cix_acpi_resource_lookup_ready);
 
+/*
+ * cix_acpi_resource_lookup_defer() only converts -ENOENT into
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER while readiness is false, so once CIXA1019 actually
+ * probes, consumers stop deferring. But if CIXA1019 never probes at
+ * all - its ACPI device is absent from this platform's namespace, its
+ * driver failed to register, or (in principle, since the symbol is
+ * bool-only today - see the header) it is ever made tristate and never
+ * loaded - readiness would otherwise never be published, and any
+ * consumer relying on cix_acpi_resource_lookup_defer() would defer
+ * forever instead of eventually seeing its real error.
+ *
+ * Bound that wait explicitly: starting at late_initcall (by which
+ * point every built-in driver's initial probe attempt, including any
+ * ACPI-namespace-order-dependent ones, has already run at least once),
+ * poll for CIXA1019's presence and for readiness a fixed, small number
+ * of times. If CIXA1019 is not present in the ACPI namespace at all,
+ * publish readiness immediately - nothing will ever inject properties,
+ * so there is nothing further to wait for. If it is present but still
+ * has not published readiness after the bounded wait, force readiness
+ * anyway and log loudly: something is wrong (a real bug elsewhere), but
+ * an unbounded consumer defer is worse than surfacing the consumers'
+ * real errors at that point.
+ *
+ * These constants are a software boot-policy choice (how long we are
+ * willing to wait during boot before giving up), not a hardware timing
+ * requirement - unlike PHY/reset sequencing delays, they are not meant
+ * to model any register/electrical characteristic.
+ */
+#define CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_MAX_WAIT_RETRIES 20
+#define CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_WAIT_INTERVAL msecs_to_jiffies(250)
+
+static void acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_work(struct work_struct *work);
+static DECLARE_DELAYED_WORK(acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_dwork,
+			     acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_work);
+static unsigned int acpi_resource_lookup_wait_retries;
+
+static void acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	if (smp_load_acquire(&res_lookup_ready))
+		return; /* CIXA1019 completed injection on its own already. */
+
+	if (!acpi_dev_present("CIXA1019", NULL, -1)) {
+		pr_info("cix-acpi-resource-lookup: CIXA1019 not present in ACPI namespace; unblocking any deferred consumers\n");
+		smp_store_release(&res_lookup_ready, true);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (++acpi_resource_lookup_wait_retries >=
+	    CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_MAX_WAIT_RETRIES) {
+		pr_warn("cix-acpi-resource-lookup: CIXA1019 is present but has not completed injection after %u ms - forcing readiness so waiting consumers do not defer forever; their reset/clock/resource-name lookups may now fail instead\n",
+			jiffies_to_msecs(CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_MAX_WAIT_RETRIES *
+					  CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_WAIT_INTERVAL));
+		smp_store_release(&res_lookup_ready, true);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	schedule_delayed_work(&acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_dwork,
+			      CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_WAIT_INTERVAL);
+}
+
+static int __init acpi_resource_lookup_late_init(void)
+{
+	if (acpi_disabled || smp_load_acquire(&res_lookup_ready))
+		return 0;
+
+	schedule_delayed_work(&acpi_resource_lookup_force_ready_dwork,
+			      CIX_ACPI_RESOURCE_LOOKUP_WAIT_INTERVAL);
+	return 0;
+}
+late_initcall(acpi_resource_lookup_late_init);
+
 static int acpi_resource_lookup_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	acpi_walk_namespace(ACPI_TYPE_ANY, ACPI_ROOT_OBJECT,
